      "writtenOn": "2026-10-15T16:07:56Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "For `repo upload`, `abort_if_user_denies` is `True`, so skipping the prompt does not silently skip running the hooks—instead, it raises `HookError(\"You must allow the pre-upload hook (use --verify to run without prompting) or use --no-verify.\")` and aborts the upload. (`post-sync` in `repo sync` has `abort_if_user_denies\u003dFalse`, but `repo sync` doesn\u0027t have a `-y`/`--yes` flag, so `self._yes` is always `False` there.)\n\nThat said, for a user who runs `repo upload -y` in a new checkout where hooks haven\u0027t been approved yet, this will fail fast with that error message instead of prompting `(yes/always/NO)?`. The motivation here is that AI agents (which often run commands in a PTY where `isatty()` is `True`) pass `repo upload -y` and currently hang forever on `input()`.\n\nDo you think failing fast with the `--verify` / `--no-verify` message when `-y` is passed is okay, or would you prefer a different way to handle non-interactive / AI agent invocations?",
